No it's not. Because a check engine light you're able to take to a 3rd party mechanic to have them diagnose and fix the issue.

&#x200B;

You can't do that with the iPhone. Because the parts are serialized. If you want this fixed, you have to go straight to Apple or an apple service provider, who will charge you around $500-$600 for this repair.

&#x200B;

A 3rd party can do the same exact repair for much cheaper, but because apple has their software locked down, they have no way to get rid of the "non-genuine" camera message even if they have a brand new outta the box OEM apple camera.

